---
/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   / — the free AI Opportunity Brief offer, and the site's front door.

   IT LEADS WITH THE BRIEF because the brief is the bottom rung. It costs a
   visitor four fields and no calendar, where the Reality Check costs them an
   hour on a call with strangers. The front door used to sell the hour and list
   the brief in the nav, which asked the most of someone who knew the least
   about us. The hour is now the step after, on /reality-check.

   The path this route took: /exposure read as fear-mongering, and a URL is the
   first words a visitor sees. /report was accurate and dull, and it promised
   research when the thing being given away is advice. /brief redirects here.

   TOP OF THE LADDER, and a different promise from the Reality Check. This one
   is free and automated: we read your public pages, find what comparable
   companies have actually done, and write you an analysis. The Reality Check is
   the hour that follows.

   THE COPY SELLS THE ANALYSIS, NOT THE CITATIONS. The first version of this
   brief led with sourced facts and a reader's verdict was "a dump of press
   release headlines — ChatGPT could have spit this out". They were right, and
   the fix was upstream. So this page promises what the brief now answers:
   where you stand, where you're strong, where you're exposed, what companies
   like you did and whether it worked, and what to weigh. Sourcing is the proof,
   not the pitch.

   The call is framed as course-correcting the brief rather than as a sales
   call, because that is what it honestly is: the document is built entirely
   from outside, the boundary section lists what we could not see, and the
   fastest way to fix that is an hour with the people who know.

   Positioning: written to the recommended position in docs/01 — design-led AI
   product studio with a care-operations wedge — and deliberately naming no
   vertical, so landing Decision #1 does not invalidate the page.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import ExposureRequest from '../components/ExposureRequest.astro';

const title = 'Your free AI Opportunity Brief — SeeSaw Labs';
const description =
  'Three or four AI ideas for your business that nobody has pitched you yet, each one sized so you can judge it. Researched from your surface, your category and your competitors\u2019 actual moves. Free, and yours to keep.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <!-- ------------------------------ hero ------------------------------ -->
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero__in">
      <p class="eyebrow">Free · No catch · Yours to keep</p>
      <h1 class="hero__h">
        The AI ideas for your business<br />
        <span class="hero__em">that nobody has pitched you yet.</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="lede hero__lede">
        Three or four things we&rsquo;d build if this were our company, each
        one sized so you can judge whether it&rsquo;s worth the effort. Plus
        what we&rsquo;d want to know before starting. It&rsquo;s the first
        working session of a strategic engagement, done before you&rsquo;ve
        spent anything.
      </p>
      <div class="hero__cta">
        <a href="#request" class="btn">Get my brief</a>
        <a href="#inside" class="btn btn--ghost">What's in it</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ---------------------------- the problem ------------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--sunk">
    <div class="wrap-narrow">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why this exists</p>
      <h2 class="h2">
        Plenty of people will tell you AI matters. Far fewer will tell you what
        to do about it in <em>your</em> business.
      </h2>
      <p class="body">
        You&rsquo;ve read the generic version already. The specific version
        takes real research. Before we write a word, we crawl your public
        surface the way a buyer reads it, sweep the web for what comparable
        companies have actually shipped and when, resolve each of those to a
        dated source we can hand you, pull live search-demand data for your
        category, and read the regulatory and funding movement around your
        sector. Then we do the part that isn&rsquo;t research: decide what
        we&rsquo;d build first, and why.
      </p>
      <p class="body">
        That whole pipeline is something we built and run ourselves, which is
        why we can point it at your company before you&rsquo;ve paid us
        anything. It&rsquo;s also the most honest way to be judged &mdash; on
        the quality of the thinking, with the sources attached, rather than on
        a deck about our process.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- --------------------------- what's inside ------------------------ -->
  <section class="section" id="inside">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">What's in it</p>
        <h2 class="h2">What you actually get</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Six sections. The ideas and their numbers up front, the research they
          came from at the back, so any line of it can be checked.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Where we think you stand</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            A short read on your position, built from the research: your own
            surface, your category, and the companies you get compared to.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Three or four ideas we&rsquo;d build</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Specific enough to disagree with, in the order we&rsquo;d do them.
            Not &ldquo;add AI to your operation&rdquo;.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">What each one might be worth</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Arithmetic you can follow, so you can tell a serious idea from an
            expensive one before committing a quarter to it.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">The questions we&rsquo;d ask first</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            The few answers that would change our recommendation. Useful even
            if you never reply &mdash; they are the questions worth asking
            internally.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">What your competitors already shipped</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            Their real AI moves, dated and sourced, with what each one suggests
            about where your category is heading. If nobody has moved yet,
            that is the finding.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3 class="card__h">Where your context sharpens it</h3>
          <p class="card__b">
            The specific facts that would take a recommendation from directional
            to costed. Bring those and the second version is a plan.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------ the honesty promise --------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--sunk">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How we work</p>
        <h2 class="h2">The same discipline we&rsquo;d bring to the build</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="two">
        <div>
          <p class="body">
            Every number in your brief either links to the source we found it
            in, or is an assumption we state with the reasoning attached. Where
            only you can know the figure, it stays a
            visible <span class="blankdemo">blank</span> rather than a
            confident guess. That is enforced in the pipeline, not in a style
            guide: a claim we cannot trace never reaches you.
          </p>
          <p class="body">
            It is how we work on paid engagements too. Reasoning in the open,
            assumptions labelled, so the people who would have to live with a
            decision can test it rather than take it on trust. A
            recommendation you cannot inspect is one you cannot act on.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <p class="body">
            The brief is deliberately the outside view. It sees your public
            surface, your category, and what your competitors have shipped. It
            does not see your volumes, your cycle times, or which manual step
            is quietly costing you the most.
          </p>
          <p class="body">
            That is what the hour is for. You bring the operating detail, we
            bring a version of this document that is costed and sequenced.
            Most of our engagements begin at exactly that point: a shared view
            of the work, arrived at together, before anyone signs anything.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="samplelink">
        <!-- Points at the brief we ran on ourselves. /sample is the Reality
             Check assessment, a different document, and linking there from here
             showed a visitor the structure of a deliverable nobody asked for. -->
        <a href="/sample-brief">Read the one we ran on ourselves &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------ request ---------------------------- -->
  <section class="section" id="request">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Four fields, no calendar</p>
        <h2 class="h2">Point us at the right things</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The research goes wherever you send it. Two answers steer all of it:
          how you&rsquo;d describe the business in one line, and who you
          actually count as a competitor. Get those right and the brief is
          about your market rather than your industry.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ExposureRequest />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ------------------------------- close ---------------------------- -->
  <section class="section section--deep">
    <div class="wrap-narrow close">
      <p class="eyebrow">And then</p>
      <h2 class="close__h">Turn it into a plan.</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        An hour with the two people who would lead the work. You fill in what
        we could not see, we sequence the ideas against your actual capacity,
        and you leave with a written assessment of what to do first. That hour
        is the AI Reality Check. It is free as well, and the assessment is
        yours whether or not you work with us.
      </p>
      <div class="close__ctas">
        <a href="/book" class="btn btn--on-deep">Book the hour</a>
        <a href="/reality-check" class="close__more">What the hour covers &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <style>
    /* Local to this page. Shared primitives live in styles/tokens.css. */
    .hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--sp-16), 10vw, var(--sp-24)) var(--sp-16); }
    .hero__in { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-5); }
    .hero__h {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-4xl), 6.5vw, var(--text-6xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: 0;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
    .hero__em { color: var(--accent); }
    .hero__lede { max-width: 56ch; margin: 0; }
    .hero__cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-3); margin-top: var(--sp-2); }
    .hero__fine { font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--muted); margin: 0; }

    .h2 {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-2xl), 3.6vw, var(--text-4xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: var(--sp-3) 0 var(--sp-5);
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
    .h2 em { font-style: italic; color: var(--accent); }
    .section--deep .h2 { color: #fff; }

    .head { max-width: 60ch; margin-bottom: var(--sp-10); }
    .head .lede { margin: 0; }

    .body {
      font-size: var(--text-lg);
      line-height: var(--lh-relaxed);
      color: var(--ink-2);
      max-width: 60ch;
      margin: 0 0 var(--sp-5);
    }
    .body:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

    .cards {
      list-style: none;
      counter-reset: card;
      margin: 0;
      padding: 0;
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--sp-5);
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    }
    .card {
      counter-increment: card;
      position: relative;
      background: var(--surface);
      border: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
      padding: var(--sp-6);
    }
    .card::before {
      content: counter(card, decimal-leading-zero);
      font-family: var(--font-accent);
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--accent);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-wide);
    }
    .card__h {
      font-size: var(--text-xl);
      font-weight: 600;
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      line-height: var(--lh-snug);
      margin: var(--sp-2) 0 var(--sp-3);
    }
    .card__b { font-size: var(--text-md); line-height: var(--lh-relaxed); color: var(--ink-2); margin: 0; }

    .two { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-8); }
    @media (min-width: 48rem) {
      .two { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: var(--sp-10); }
    }

    /* Mirrors how a declared blank renders inside the brief itself. */
    .blankdemo {
      font-family: var(--font-mono);
      font-size: 0.9em;
      color: var(--accent-deep);
      background: var(--accent-soft);
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent);
      padding: 0.05rem 0.3rem;
      border-radius: var(--radius-sm) var(--radius-sm) 0 0;
      white-space: nowrap;
    }

    .samplelink { margin: var(--sp-10) 0 0; font-size: var(--text-lg); }
    .samplelink a { color: var(--accent-deep); font-weight: 500; }

    .close { display: grid; gap: var(--sp-5); justify-items: start; }
    .close__ctas { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--sp-5); align-items: center; }
    .close__more { color: inherit; opacity: .82; font-size: var(--text-base); }
    .close__h {
      font-size: clamp(var(--text-3xl), 5vw, var(--text-5xl));
      font-weight: 600;
      line-height: var(--lh-tight);
      letter-spacing: var(--ls-tight);
      margin: 0;
      color: #fff;
      text-wrap: balance;
    }
  </style>
</Base>
